# Frontend UI Conventions

- Use functional React components and TypeScript. Export reusable UI components by name and suffix prop types with `Props`.
- Keep each reusable component with its CSS Module and behavior tests. Split files only when it makes the component easier to read.
- Build only the variants required by current callers. Reuse existing components before adding wrappers or abstractions.
- Use semantic design tokens for color, typography, spacing, radius, shadow, and motion. Do not add arbitrary visual values inside component styles.
- Use CSS Modules for component styles and `data-*` attributes for finite visual variants and states.
- Use Base UI only when its interaction and accessibility behavior saves real code. Use native HTML for simple elements.
- Use Tabler Icons for interface icons. Icon-only controls must have an accessible name.
- Keep state as close as possible to where it is used. Do not add a global state library without cross-feature shared state that React cannot reasonably own.
- Prefer controlled props for form values and validation state. Internal state is only for private UI behavior such as password visibility.
- Derive values during render instead of storing duplicate state. Use effects only to synchronize with systems outside React.
- Callers own async and business state. Components receive states such as `loading`, `disabled`, and `error`; they do not perform authentication or API calls.
- Connect labels, descriptions, and errors with stable IDs. Preserve keyboard navigation, focus visibility, and native semantics.
- Test user-visible behavior and accessibility with Testing Library. Avoid assertions against implementation details or CSS class names.
- Twenty is a structural and visual reference. Do not copy code from `twenty-front`; reuse substantial `twenty-ui` MIT code only with its required license notice.
